we use GROUP BY clause + HAVING claue
----> to filter grouped rows
HAVING clause has the same type of constraint written like in WHERE clause

#Qeury
- SELECT group_by_column, AGG_FUNC (column_expression) AS aggregate_result_alia, ....
- From mytable
- WHERE condition
- GROUP BY column
- HAVING group_condition;

## Exercise-1
--> Find the number of Artists in the studio (Without Having clause)
- SELECT role, count(Name) as number_of_artist
- FROM Employees
- WHERE role= 'Artist';

##Exercise-2
--> Find the number of employees of each role in the studio
- SELECT role, count(name) AS number_of_employees
- FROM employees
- Group by role;

##Exercise- 3
--> Find the total number of years employed by all engineers
- SELECT role, sum(Years_Employed)
- FROM Employees
- GROUP BY role
- HAVING role= 'Engineer';

